DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     vveelllluumm is the drawing board of this workstation: electronic
     schematics, block diagrams, flowcharts, network maps,
     software structure charts and figures for manuals, with
     clean printed output and full membership in the Unix
     pipeline.  It must be run under the zzvviieeww desktop and ac-
     cepts the standard window _o_p_t_i_o_n_s of GUI applications; see
     zzvviieeww. The optional _f_i_l_e argument names a drawing to open.

     The file name sits in its own bar at the top, with a `*'
     while the drawing has unsaved changes; the TOOLBAR row of
     cells sits under it, and the palette down the left edge
     holds the symbol bank.  Symbols come from LIBRARY FILES
     named in /usr/vellum/etc/libs: ddiissccrreettee (resistor through
     opamp), llooggiicc (gates and flip-flops), ffllooww (flowchart sten-
     cils), nneett (network-map stencils), aarrcchh (software structure
     stencils), ppiidd (process / P&ID stencils), ppoowweerr (one-line
     electrical) and ppllaann (floor-plan stencils), plus the user
     scratch library /usr/vellum/etc/symbols when it holds any-
     thing.  Every loaded symbol stays resolvable at once - a
     drawing may mix libraries - but the palette shows ONE group
     at a time: the header cell above the bank names it and
     clicking the header switches to the next.  After the last
     library comes the sshhaappeess pseudo-group: the nine STRETCHABLE
     shapes (box, rounded box, diamond, oval, parallelogram,
     drum, document, circle, ellipse), the four CONNECTOR styles
     (hv, line, arrow, harrow), the Arc tool, the Net name marker
     and the Dim (dimension) tool.  The toolbar's LLiibb cell lists
     the libraries on file in a scrollable dialog, and EEddiitt opens
     the current library in the ssyymmeeddiitt symbol editor.

